course: everyday
lesson: 211
title: Growing, seeing and hearing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every body grows at its own pace: growth spurts come at different ages, so friends of the same age can be very different heights.
explain: If you cannot see the board clearly at school, you tell a teacher and a parent, because you may need an eye test and perhaps glasses.
explain: A hearing aid is a small device that helps a person hear; you respect it and never touch it.
explain: A boy in Halifax squints at the board and sits closer and closer; his teacher notices, he has an eye test, and with new glasses the letters are sharp again.
explain: Being shorter, taller, wearing glasses or a hearing aid is normal, and nobody should be teased for it.
fact: Everyone grows at their own pace.
fact: Growth spurts come at different ages, so children of the same age can be very different heights.
fact: A short child may grow a lot later, and a tall child may stop growing earlier.
fact: If you cannot see the board at school, you tell a teacher and a parent.
fact: Trouble seeing may mean you need an eye test.
fact: Glasses help many children see clearly.
fact: A hearing aid helps a person hear.
fact: You respect someone's hearing aid and do not touch it.
fact: It helps a person who wears a hearing aid if you face them and speak clearly.
fact: Differences in height, glasses and hearing aids are normal and never a reason to tease.
exercise: You are the shortest in your class and worry about it. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own pace | You will never grow any taller than you are now | Short children are always less healthy | Height is fixed for life by age nine
exercise: Why are children of the same age often different heights? | Growth spurts come at different ages | Some children forget to grow | Only tall children eat vegetables | Height depends on your birthday month
exercise: Your friend is much taller than you at ten. What might happen later? | You may catch up when your own growth spurt comes | You will always be shorter, because that never changes | Your friend will shrink | Nothing can change after age ten
exercise: You cannot read the words on the board from your desk. What do you do? | Tell your teacher and a parent | Copy from your neighbour forever | Squint harder and say nothing | Guess the words
exercise: Why should you tell someone if the board looks blurry? | You may need an eye test | The teacher will rewrite it bigger forever | The board just needs a good clean | It shows you are tired of school
exercise: What can an eye test lead to? | Glasses that help you see clearly | A new seat in the hall for every assembly | A day off school every week | A smaller board
exercise: A new classmate wears a hearing aid. What do you do? | Respect it and do not touch it | Ask to try it on | Pull it out to see how it works | Talk very quietly near it
exercise: What does a hearing aid do? | It helps the person hear | It plays music for them to enjoy | It stops them hearing noise | It is just a bit of jewellery
exercise: How can you help a friend with a hearing aid understand you? | Face them and speak clearly | Shout loudly right into their ear | Talk to them with your back turned | Cover your mouth when talking
exercise: A boy laughs at a girl for wearing glasses. What is true? | Glasses are normal and never a reason to tease | Glasses mean she is not as clever as the others | Only old people should wear glasses | She should take them off at school to fit in better
exercise: Your sister grew 8 centimetres this year but you grew only 3. What is true? | Children grow at different speeds at different times | Something is surely wrong with you and you need medicine | You will stop growing now | She is eating your food
exercise: You keep getting headaches from squinting at the board. Who should you tell? | A teacher and a parent | Nobody, it will pass on its own in a week | Only your best friend at school | The school cook at lunch
test: A girl in Vermont sits in the front row but still cannot read the board. What is the likely next step? | Telling her parents and having an eye test | Moving to a different school with bigger whiteboards | Giving up reading | Sitting even closer each week
test: Two ten-year-old twins in Glasgow are different heights. Why is that normal? | Each body grows at its own pace | Twins must be the same height or be ill | One twin is older by a whole year | Heights only ever differ in adults
test: Your classmate's hearing aid falls on the floor. What do you do? | Tell your classmate or the teacher and let them pick it up | Put it in your own ear to test it | Kick it to the side | Keep it as a joke
test: Which is true about growth spurts? | They come at different ages for different children | They happen to everyone on the morning of their tenth birthday | They only happen to boys | They happen only once a year in summer
test: A boy is teased for being the tallest in his class. What should others know? | Height differences are normal and never a reason to tease | He must be much older | Tall children are always clumsy | He should try to grow less
test: Why might a child who cannot see the board start doing worse at school? | They miss what is written, so an eye test can really help | Blurry boards make you less clever over time, so marks drop | Seeing badly is always their fault | Eye tests make reading harder
test: You want to tell a joke to a friend with a hearing aid in a noisy hall. What helps most? | Face them and speak clearly | Whisper it behind their back | Shout to them from across the hall | Write nothing down and just hope
test: Your friend is the shortest in class and is sad. What is true and kind to say? | Everyone grows at their own pace, and you may grow later | You will be short forever | Eat more sweets to grow | Stand on tiptoes all the time
test: cant read the board from the back, its all blurry | Tell your teacher and a parent; you may need an eye test | Squint harder every day | Copy from your friend forever | It's normal, ignore it
test: im the shortest in the class, will i always be? | Not necessarily, everyone grows at their own pace | Yes, you'll stay short forever, it's in your blood | Yes, height never changes after 9 | Only if your shoes are small
test: Tom's got a hearing aid, can i have a look at it | Respect it and don't touch it | Yes, take it out to look | Yes, try it on yourself | Yes, but only when he's not looking
test: kids laughin at Amy's new glasses | Differences like glasses are normal, never a reason to tease | Join in, glasses are funny | Hide her glasses for a joke | Tell her not to wear them
explain: A boy in Australia listened to music very loud through earbuds every night; by his teens he had a ringing in his ears that never went away.
explain: A girl in Ohio could not see the board well but did not tell anyone; her marks dropped for a year until an eye test showed she just needed glasses.
fact: Loud sound for a long time can damage hearing for life, and the damage cannot be repaired.
fact: Never push anything like a pencil or hairgrip into your ear; it can hurt the eardrum, the thin skin deep inside the ear that lets you hear, so tell an adult if something is stuck.
fact: Many children who struggle in class simply cannot see or hear well, and a check-up can fix it.
test: earbuds on full blast all day, it's fine i'm young | No, loud sound can damage hearing for good | Yes, young ears can't be hurt by any loud music | Yes, as long as you like the song | Only one ear at a time
test: Tom squints at the board and his marks are falling. What should happen? | He tells an adult and gets his eyes checked | He moves to sit further back, away from the glare | He copies from his neighbour | He ignores it, eyes fix themselves
test: something's stuck in my ear, gonna get it out with a pencil | No, you could hurt your eardrum, tell an adult | Yes, carefully | Use a hairgrip instead | Shake your head hard
test: did u hear that kid with the hearing aid, someone pulled it out as a joke and it broke and cost like a million quid. real lesson? | Never touch someone's hearing aid; respect it | Hearing aids are basically toys, so it's okay to try one on | It's fine if you put it back quickly | Only grown-ups' hearing aids matter
test: my mate says if u listen to music loud in ur headphones u go totally deaf in a week. what's probably blown up? | A week; but long loud sound can damage hearing for life | That loud sound can harm hearing | That hearing damage is serious | That headphones can be loud
test: everyone's saying the short boy in our class has something wrong with him cos he's tiny. pass it on? | No, everyone grows at their own pace; don't spread it | Yes, so a doctor finds out | Yes, it's just being honest | Yes, if you say it kindly
exam: Identify the most accurate statement concerning the growth of children of the same age. | Growth spurts occur at different ages, so heights differ greatly | All children of the same age attain very nearly the same height in the end | Short children never grow taller later | Tall children continue growing longest
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate action where a pupil is unable to see the board? | To inform a teacher and a parent, as an eye test may be needed | To sit nearer and inform nobody | To copy from a neighbour's book | To strain the eyes until they adapt
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning prolonged exposure to loud sound. | It may damage hearing permanently, beyond repair | It strengthens hearing over time, as a muscle is strengthened | It damages hearing only temporarily | It is harmless through headphones
exam: Ascertain the manner most helpful when addressing a person who wears a hearing aid. | Facing the person and speaking clearly | Shouting directly into the ear fitted with the aid | Speaking from behind the person | Whispering so as not to be too loud
